\DOC X86_BIGSTEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_BIGSTEP_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
On a goal whose conclusion is {(p ==> ensures x86 P Q C) ==> eventually x86 R
s}, this tactic discharges the {ensures} antecedent by instantiating it at the
current state, re-describing the state assumptions in terms of a fresh
successor state named by the string argument, and leaving the remaining
{eventually} obligation.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is a pair {(execth1,(unknown)) : thm * (thm option array)},
an execution record for the program; its theorem component is a
{LENGTH}-of-code-list equation, used to relate the goal's code and offsets. The
second argument {sname : string} names the new state variable introduced for
the post-state of the embedded {ensures}. The tactic discharges the
implication's antecedent {ensures ...} against the goal: the assumptions
describing the current state (program loaded, register and memory reads, the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE ... s0 s} relation, flag conditions) are deleted and
replaced by assumptions about the new state {s'} named by {sname}, namely the
post-condition conjuncts of the {ensures} (with existentially quantified
components stripped and their witnesses added as assumptions, say {z1 ... zp}),
the frame-preserved reads carried over, and an enlarged {MAYCHANGE ... s0 s'}
assumption; the conclusion becomes the {eventually} statement at {s'}.
{
   A ?- (t ==> ensures x86 P Q C) ==> eventually x86 R s
   ================================================  X86_BIGSTEP_TAC (execth1,(unknown)) sname
   A' ?- eventually x86 R s'
}
\noindent Sometimes it additionally emits, as the *first* of two subgoals, a
side condition arising from matching the {ensures} precondition against the
current state — typically a {bytes_loaded} claim at a shifted code address
and/or an event-list equation — stated under the original unchanged
assumptions, with the transformed goal above as the second subgoal.

\FAILURE
Fails when the {ensures} antecedent of the goal cannot be matched against the
goal's {eventually} conclusion: in the observed cases the antecedent's own
hypothesis list (the conjunction guarding the {ensures}) does not line up with
what the tactic expects, so the internal modus-ponens step finds no match and
raises a matching failure.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces the tactic yields one or two subgoals and never closes
the goal.

\ENDDOC
